{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and often acted as a mediator between different factions. In 1174 he became regent for Baldwin IV the Leper King of Jerusalem. Raymond III governed the kingdom during Baldwin IVs minority and continued to exert influence after the king came of age.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond IIIs regency was marked by conflicts with factions led by Miles of Plancy and later by Raymonds rival Guy of Lusignan. Raymond III opposed Guy of Lusignans rise to power and was involved in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where the young king Baldwin IV led a victory against Saladin. Raymond III also negotiated truces with Saladin to secure the borders of the Crusader states.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a male heir. His death left Tripoli without a clear leader at a critical time as Saladin launched a major offensive against the Crusader states. Raymond IIIs legacy includes his efforts to maintain stability in the region and his role in the complex power struggles of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His death marked the beginning of a period of decline for the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.17142000794410706}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.15024539828300476}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.389413465512916e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.015496321953833103